{title:Title}

{pstd}{bf:cinterpolate} {hline 2} C-accelerated linear and cubic interpolation (ctools)

{marker syntax}{...}

{title:Syntax}

{p 8 12 2}{cmd:cinterpolate} {it:yvar xvar} {ifin}, {opt gen:erate(newvar)}
[{opt by(varlist)} {opt ep:olate} {opt cub:ic} {opt threads(#)} {opt verbose}]

{marker description}{...}

{title:Description}

{* ctools-credit:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{pstd}
{cmd:cinterpolate} reimplements Stata's {cmd:ipolate} ({cmd:ipolate.ado}
1.3.5, April 23, 2020) and {cmd:cipolate} 1.0.0 (July 4, 2002) by Nicholas J.
Cox. See {help cinterpolate##acknowledgments:Acknowledgments} for details.
{* ctools-credit:end}{...}

{pstd}Defaults to the linear interpolation performed by {help ipolate};
{opt cubic} selects local cubic interpolation as in Nicholas J. Cox's SSC
{help cipolate}. {cmd:cinterpolate} does not call {cmd:ipolate} or require the
SSC {cmd:cipolate} package. Within each by-group, nonmissing y values at the
same x are averaged. Missing y values between known points are interpolated.
Missing x values and observations outside if/in produce missing results. The
output is double precision and the input observation order is preserved.
Without epolate, results outside the known range are missing. For cubic
interpolation, additional gaps near the ends remain missing unless two
distinct known x points are available on each side.

{marker options}{...}

{title:Options}

{pstd}{opt generate()} names the required new output variable.

{pstd}{opt by()} computes separate interpolations for numeric or string groups.
The {cmd:by:} prefix is also accepted; do not combine it with by(). Extended missing
group values remain distinct. {cmd:strL} groups may hold text of any length
and binary values.

{pstd}{opt epolate} extrapolates at both ends using the nearest two distinct
known x points. A group with only one known point cannot extrapolate.

{pstd}{opt cubic} fits a cubic polynomial through the nearest two distinct
known x points on each side of a missing value. The result is missing unless
there are at least two distinct known x values on each side. Cubic
interpolation fits a polynomial through these four points; interpolated y
values can lie outside the range of the known y values. For repeated x values,
the nonmissing y values are averaged before selecting interpolation points.
Extended missing values in x and y are treated as missing. The options
{opt cubic} and {opt epolate} cannot be combined.

{pstd}{opt threads(#)} sets the maximum number of threads, from 1 to 256.
Omitting it or specifying zero uses the {help ctools##threads:ctools setting}.

{pstd}{opt verbose} reports loading, sorting, interpolation, and storage times.

{marker results}{...}

{title:Stored results}

{pstd}As {cmd:ipolate} does, {cmd:cinterpolate} leaves the number of missing
values in the new variable in {cmd:r(N)} and reports it as
"({it:#} missing value(s) generated)" when it is not zero.

{synoptset 15 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 15 19 2: Scalars}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(N)}}number of missing values generated{p_end}

{marker examples}{...}

{title:Examples}

{phang2}{cmd:. cinterpolate income year, generate(income_i) by(person)}{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. bysort person: cinterpolate income year, generate(income_e) epolate}{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. cinterpolate income year, generate(income_c) by(person) cubic}{p_end}

{title:Remarks}

{pstd}The command changes neither the source variables nor observation order.
The output variable must be new. Interpolation uses the selected sample only;
{cmd:if}/{cmd:in} excludes observations from both the known points and the output.


{pstd}For scripts using the former ctools linear command, replace
{cmd:cipolate} with {cmd:cinterpolate}. No ctools alias named {cmd:cipolate}
is provided, so the SSC command can coexist. Remove stale ctools
{cmd:cipolate.ado} and {cmd:cipolate.sthlp} files when updating an existing
installation; locate them with {cmd:which cipolate} and inspect their headers
before removal.


{* ctools-attribution:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{marker acknowledgments}{...}
{title:Acknowledgments}

{pstd}
{cmd:cinterpolate} reimplements two commands. By default it follows
{cmd:ipolate}, an official Stata command. With the {cmd:cubic} option it
follows {cmd:cipolate}, written by Nicholas J. Cox (Durham University).

{pstd}
{cmd:cinterpolate} is pinned to these versions:

{p2colset 9 27 29 2}{...}
{p2col:Command}{cmd:ipolate} (official Stata){p_end}
{p2col:Authors}StataCorp LLC{p_end}
{p2col:Version}{cmd:ipolate.ado} 1.3.5{p_end}
{p2col:Date}April 23, 2020{p_end}
{p2col:Header line}{cmd:*! version 1.3.5  23apr2020}{p_end}
{p2col:Shipped with}StataNow 19.5 (revision 14 Oct 2026){p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{p2colset 9 27 29 2}{...}
{p2col:Command}{cmd:cipolate}{p_end}
{p2col:Authors}Nicholas J. Cox{p_end}
{p2col:Version}1.0.0{p_end}
{p2col:Date}July 4, 2002{p_end}
{p2col:Header line}{cmd:*! NJC 1.0.0 4 July 2002}{p_end}
{p2col:Source}SSC ({cmd:ssc install cipolate}), distributed October 16,
2005.{p_end}
{p2col:Latest version?}Yes, as of November 25, 2026.{p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{pstd}
{cmd:cinterpolate} changes only when ctools is updated to newer versions of
these commands and tested against them. {cmd:which} shows the version of each
command installed on your machine. {help ctools##acknowledgments:help ctools}
lists the pinned versions of all ctools commands.

{pstd}
Please report bugs and questions about {cmd:cinterpolate} at
{browse "https://github.com/mdroste/stata-ctools/issues":github.com/mdroste/stata-ctools/issues}.
StataCorp and the authors of {cmd:cipolate} do not maintain
{cmd:cinterpolate}. Undocumented differences between {cmd:cinterpolate} and
the original commands are bugs in {cmd:cinterpolate}.

{pstd}
The help file of {cmd:cipolate} cites Hamming (1973), Morton (1964), and Press
et al. (1992) for the cubic method.

{pstd}
If you use {cmd:cinterpolate} in published work, please cite the original
command:

{phang2}Cox, N. J. 2002. CIPOLATE: Stata module for cubic interpolation.
Statistical Software Components S426202, Boston College Department of
Economics. Revised October 16, 2005.{p_end}
